# Engineering Rules

## Tool Provisioning
- Use the project-pinned or already-provisioned CRAP, mutation, DRY, and APS tools. Check availability once per long-lived role session, not once per task.
- `swarmforge/toolchain.lock.json` is authoritative for Node, Babashka, TypeScript, Codex capabilities, vendored APS, and optional Clojure analysis tools. Use `node scripts/check-swarmforge-toolchain.mjs` for offline validation and its explicit `--provision <tool>` mode only when a required runtime or tool is absent.
- Do not contact GitHub merely to refresh a working tool. Refresh versions only during an explicit tool-maintenance task, then pin the resolved revision in project configuration.
- If a required pinned tool is unavailable, report that single provisioning blocker instead of repeatedly retrying network installs from inside the sandbox.
- Language tool table:
  - TypeScript: use the repository-pinned TypeScript compiler, architecture checker, coverage, and any explicitly pinned analysis tools; do not substitute Clojure tools for TypeScript production code.
  - Go: install with `go install`; mutation `github.com/unclebob/mutate4go`, CRAP `github.com/unclebob/crap4go`, DRY `github.com/unclebob/dry4go`.
  - Clojure: use Clojure CLI/deps.edn; provision the locked local checkouts for mutation (`clj-mutate`), CRAP (`crap4clj`), and DRY (`dry4clj`) through the toolchain checker before first use.
  - Java: install with Maven (`mvn`); mutation `github.com/unclebob/mutate4java`, CRAP `github.com/unclebob/crap4java`, DRY `github.com/unclebob/dry4java`.

## Language Defaults
- For Clojure projects, prefer Babashka where possible.
- For Clojure projects, prefer Speclj for unit and behavior tests.
- For Clojure or Babashka projects using Speclj, use `github.com/unclebob/speclj-structure-check` to validate test syntax. If a Speclj spec file changed, run the structure check before executing the relevant test command.
- For Java projects, avoid using Maven to run tests; build dedicated test runners and run those instead.

## Design And Testability
- Work in small, reviewable increments.
- Prefer the simplest design that supports the current behavior and leaves clear options for the next step.
- Keep tests close to the behavior being changed.
- Separate testable modules from environmentally unsuitable modules that open GUIs, depend on external devices, throw environment errors, emit system errors, or hang under automated tests. Maximize testable code and minimize the unsuitable boundary.
- Only testable modules should participate in tools that run tests, including unit tests, acceptance tests, coverage, mutation testing, CRAP analysis, DRY analysis that invokes tests, and property tests.
- Keep property tests separate from normal verification. Do not include property-test tags in normal unit coverage, Gherkin acceptance mutation, language mutation tools, CRAP, or coverage commands unless the role owns property-test verification or the user explicitly asks for property tests.

## Acceptance Pipeline
- Use the locked tree under `vendor/acceptance-pipeline-specification` for Gherkin acceptance tests. Validate its digest through the toolchain checker; do not fetch APS during ordinary role startup.
- The vendored Acceptance Pipeline Specification supplies `gherkin-parser` and `gherkin-mutator`; use those commands instead of reimplementing them in the project.
- Prefer the Babashka APS tools for `gherkin-parser`, `gherkin-mutator`, and related APS support commands.
- Use Go-based APS tools only if the Babashka APS tools do not work in the current project environment.
- Project-specific acceptance pipeline components are the acceptance entrypoint generator, acceptance runtime, project step handlers, runner adapter, and convenience scripts.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation means running `gherkin-mutator` to mutate Gherkin example values.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation runs must report periodic progress/status so agents can distinguish normal long-running work from a hang.

## Verification
- Before running language, build, or test commands, prefer project-local cache/configuration paths inside the assigned worktree. The `swarmforge/scripts/clj` wrapper keeps Clojure configuration, Maven artifacts, and gitlibs there. Avoid default cache locations that write outside the project and may trigger sandbox or permission restrictions.
- Let the focused verification runner parallelize independent unit, parser, generator, and receipt-backed pack-session leaves within its bounded worker limit. Keep Chrome adapters sequential; the runner consolidates independent pack-session failures after checkpoints complete.
- Avoid running whole-suite language test commands concurrently with acceptance generation.
- Run the relevant local verification command before handoff whenever the project has one.
- Treat verification-pack dependencies as build or contract dependencies, not permission to run their complete regression suites. Use `--with-dependencies` only when impact evidence requires it.
- Property tests are opt-in with `--property` outside terminal `--full` verification, except that durable `--prepare-evidence` checkpoints require them.
- Run the task's exact pack once on a settled candidate. During correction loops, run only the failing leaf. Do not repeat a passing exact pack unless later changes could invalidate it.
- Run direct Chrome corrections through `browser-test <registered-test-path>`. It is the narrow, approval-friendly browser boundary; never request broad danger-full-access merely to obtain localhost CDP sockets.
- Codex roles run with explicit workspace-write isolation, `on-request` approvals, and a fail-closed network proxy that permits only exact `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` destinations. Git metadata remains protected. Use precise Git commands and let the configured automatic approval reviewer handle them; do not request or simulate broad write access to `.git`.
- Own only failures in the selected pack that are attributable to the task lineage or touched boundary. Do not repair unrelated full-suite or baseline failures; record the exact leaf as unrelated and keep it out of the implementation diff.
- Downstream roles may consume inbound commit-bound Git-note evidence only while the candidate commit and tree remain unchanged. A role that creates a new candidate runs fresh evidence for every complete pack affected by its change, using the inbound commit as `base:`; verification ownership moves forward without replaying unchanged terminal suites.

## Functional Delivery And Runtime Evidence
- A task originating from an approved externally visible behavior or regression specification is functional unless it explicitly says it is test-only, documentation-only, tooling-only, or prompt-only.
- Functional delivery requires production source or delivery-asset changes that cause the behavior. Feature files, acceptance handlers, tests, mutation evidence, manifests, generated metadata, and source-string assertions are supporting evidence, not the implementation.
- Passing tests cannot convert a test-only diff into a completed functional task.
- Runtime claims must be tested at the named boundary. Browser clipboard, storage, computed styles, focus, scrolling, geometry, and rendered visibility require the running product boundary or a deterministic adapter that invokes the same production callback.
- Acceptance-world flags, self-comparison of example values, and searches for expected source strings must not be used as substitutes for runtime outcomes.

## Guardrails
- Do not edit mutation testing or Gherkin acceptance mutation manifests by hand; allow approved mutation tools to update those manifests as part of their normal runs.
- Do not commit unrelated local changes or generated artifacts unless required for the task.
- Before relying on an unfamiliar command, inspect local help or project documentation.
